An escaped bracket is text, so `- \[ \] text` is a plain item and not a
task, and an escaped marker is not a list at all.

This is the sharpest test of how the checkbox is read. Whether the box is
TICKED is taken from the first bracket pair on the item's own source line,
which is a rule about raw bytes — and these lines all hold bracket pairs
that are NOT boxes. The rule survives because the two questions are asked
separately: cmark decides whether the item has a box at all, and only then
is the line read to see which box it is. cmark's tasklist extension never
matches an escaped bracket, so the line is never scanned.

    - \[ \] ...    a list item whose text opens with brackets
    \- [ ] ...     an escaped marker, so a paragraph and not a list

The escaped marker line gains ONE escape on the first pass and then settles:
the paragraph text opens with `[`, which the CommonMark writer escapes so it
cannot be read as a link reference next time round. Nothing is lost, and the
`then-args` pass is what pins that it settles rather than drifting.
